$$x + 5y > 13$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$(4, 1)$"},{"key":"B","text":"$(2, 1)$"},{"key":"C","text":"$(1, 3)$"},{"key":"D","text":"$(5, 2)$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Each point must satisfy both $3x - 2y \\leq 7$ and $x + 5y > 13$. For $(1, 3)$: $3(1) - 2(3) = -3 \\leq 7$ is true, and $1 + 5(3) = 16 > 13$ is true, so this point is in the solution region. For $(4, 1)$: $3(4) - 2(1) = 10 \\leq 7$ is false, so it fails the first inequality. For $(2, 1)$: $3(2) - 2(1) = 4 \\leq 7$ is true, but $2 + 5(1) = 7 > 13$ is false, so it fails the second inequality. For $(5, 2)$: $3(5) - 2(2) = 11 \\leq 7$ is false, so it fails the first inequality. A common error is testing only one inequality and selecting a point that satisfies just one condition.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

















$$x - 2y = -7$$

What is the value of $x + y$ in the solution to the system of equations above?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$3$"},{"key":"B","text":"$5$"},{"key":"C","text":"$8$"},{"key":"D","text":"$11$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Adding the two equations eliminates $y$: $(3x + 2y) + (x - 2y) = 19 + (-7)$, giving $4x = 12$, so $x = 3$. Substituting into $3(3) + 2y = 19$ gives $2y = 10$, so $y = 5$. Therefore $x + y = 3 + 5 = 8$. The distractor $3$ gives only the value of $x$, and $5$ gives only the value of $y$, each representing an incomplete answer. The distractor $11$ results from an arithmetic error such as computing $4x = 12$ but then incorrectly finding $x = 4$ and $y = 7$.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"arithmetic_slip"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);










$$x - y = 4$$

What is the value of $x$ in the solution to the system of equations above?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '7', ARRAY['7'], E'Adding the two equations eliminates $y$: $(2x + y) + (x - y) = 17 + 4$, which simplifies to $3x = 21$. Dividing both sides by $3$ gives $x = 7$. Substituting back, $y = 17 - 2(7) = 3$, confirming the solution $(7, 3)$ satisfies both equations.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);








$$kx - 3y = 5$$

For what value of $k$ does the system of equations above have no solution?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$2$"},{"key":"B","text":"$3$"},{"key":"C","text":"$6$"},{"key":"D","text":"$18$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'A system has no solution when the equations represent parallel lines — same slope but different intercepts. Multiplying the second equation by $3$ gives $3kx - 9y = 15$. For the lines to be parallel, the left sides must be proportional: $3k = 6$, so $k = 2$. With $k = 2$, the system becomes $6x - 9y = 14$ and $6x - 9y = 15$, which are inconsistent (same left side, different right side), confirming no solution. Choosing $3$ reflects confusing the coefficient of $y$ with the required value of $k$. Choosing $6$ mistakes the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation for $k$. Choosing $18$ results from multiplying $6$ and $3$ instead of recognizing the proportional relationship between the equations.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





















Text 2: Environmental engineer Priya Ramachandran contends that the value of urban parks lies in their measurable ecological services rather than their psychological effects. Her research team''s three-year monitoring study in Chennai demonstrated that a single hectare of urban tree canopy intercepts approximately 37,000 liters of stormwater annually, reduces ambient air temperature by up to 2.1 degrees Celsius, and sequesters 4.8 metric tons of carbon dioxide. Without these functions, Ramachandran argues, cities would face dramatically higher infrastructure costs for drainage, cooling, and air quality management.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 1 emphasizes the mental health benefits of green spaces for residents, while Text 2 focuses on the quantifiable environmental and infrastructure services that green spaces provide."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 1 questions whether green spaces are cost-effective, while Text 2 argues they should be expanded regardless of cost."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 1 draws on historical evidence about park design, while Text 2 relies on projections about future climate conditions."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 1 argues that green spaces benefit only nearby residents, while Text 2 claims they have no effect on local temperature."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 1 frames urban green spaces as psychological refuges that reduce perceived stress and increase life satisfaction, while Text 2 frames them as providers of measurable ecological services such as stormwater interception, temperature reduction, and carbon sequestration. The option contrasting mental health benefits with environmental and infrastructure services accurately captures this difference. The option about cost-effectiveness is wrong because neither text discusses the cost-effectiveness of green spaces. The option about historical evidence and future projections is wrong because Text 1 uses a contemporary survey and Text 2 uses a three-year monitoring study, neither historical evidence nor future projections. The option claiming Text 1 says benefits apply only to nearby residents while Text 2 denies temperature effects contradicts both texts: Text 1 discusses proximity but does not limit benefits exclusively, and Text 2 explicitly demonstrates a 2.1-degree temperature reduction.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);









Text 2: Neurolinguist Henrik Sjöberg contends that bilingualism does confer measurable cognitive benefits, but only within a narrow domain: managing linguistic interference. In a 2023 experimental study, Sjöberg showed that bilinguals outperformed monolinguals specifically on tasks requiring suppression of competing lexical representations — for instance, naming a color word printed in a mismatched ink color when the word also had a translation equivalent in the participant''s other language. Sjöberg concludes that bilingual experience fine-tunes a specialized conflict-monitoring mechanism rather than enhancing domain-general executive function.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By acknowledging that Sjöberg's narrow finding is consistent with her own conclusion, since a language-specific mechanism is not the same as the generalized advantage she challenged."},{"key":"B","text":"By arguing that Sjöberg's experimental design likely suffered from the same publication bias she identified in earlier studies."},{"key":"C","text":"By rejecting Sjöberg's data on the grounds that task-switching experiments are inherently unreliable."},{"key":"D","text":"By insisting that socioeconomic confounds make all bilingualism research impossible to interpret."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Okafor''s central claim is that bilinguals do not have a generalized cognitive advantage; Sjöberg explicitly limits his finding to a specialized conflict-monitoring mechanism rather than domain-general executive function. The option about Okafor acknowledging compatibility captures the logical relationship: Sjöberg''s narrow, domain-specific finding does not contradict Okafor''s rejection of a broad executive-function advantage, so she would likely see it as consistent with her position. The option about publication bias is an overreach because Okafor attributed bias to earlier studies with inflated effect sizes, and nothing in Text 2 suggests Sjöberg''s controlled 2023 experiment suffers from the same issue. The option about rejecting task-switching experiments as unreliable misreads Text 1: Okafor did not question the reliability of executive function tasks themselves but rather argued that demographic confounds inflated the measured effects. The option about socioeconomic confounds making all research uninterpretable overstates Okafor''s position: she argued that controlling for these variables eliminated the generalized advantage, not that such controls are impossible to implement.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





- Azolla, a tiny aquatic fern, forms a symbiotic relationship with nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria (Anabaena azollae) that live inside its leaf cavities.
- Rice farmers in Vietnam and southern China have cultivated azolla as a living fertilizer for over 1,500 years by growing it on flooded paddies.
- Modern field trials show that incorporating azolla biomass into paddy soil can reduce synthetic nitrogen fertilizer use by 37 to 52 percent while maintaining comparable yields.
- Azolla doubles its biomass every 3 to 5 days under optimal conditions, making it one of the fastest-growing plants on Earth.
- Unlike synthetic fertilizers, azolla cultivation does not produce nitrous oxide, a potent greenhouse gas.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Because the cyanobacterium Anabaena azollae lives inside the leaf cavities of the aquatic fern azolla, the plant is capable of fixing atmospheric nitrogen through this symbiotic relationship."},{"key":"B","text":"Azolla, a fast-growing aquatic fern that harbors nitrogen-fixing bacteria, can replace 37 to 52 percent of synthetic fertilizer on rice paddies without reducing yields or generating nitrous oxide emissions."},{"key":"C","text":"Rice farmers in Vietnam and southern China have cultivated azolla on flooded paddies for over 1,500 years, demonstrating the plant's long history as a traditional agricultural tool."},{"key":"D","text":"Under optimal conditions, azolla doubles its biomass every 3 to 5 days, which makes it one of the fastest-growing plants on Earth and a subject of interest to biologists."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal requires two things: introducing azolla to an unfamiliar audience and presenting it as a practical alternative to synthetic fertilizer. The sentence about azolla being a fast-growing aquatic fern that can replace a significant portion of synthetic fertilizer without reducing yields or producing nitrous oxide accomplishes both — it identifies the plant for newcomers and conveys its practical advantages with specific data. The sentence about Anabaena azollae and symbiotic nitrogen fixation explains the biological mechanism but does not address practical agricultural benefits or introduce the plant accessibly. The sentence about 1,500 years of Vietnamese and Chinese cultivation emphasizes historical tradition but does not frame azolla as a practical modern alternative or introduce it to an unfamiliar reader. The sentence about biomass doubling describes growth rate and biological interest but does not connect this trait to fertilizer replacement or agricultural practicality.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 85, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


- Spider dragline silk has a tensile strength of approximately 1.1 gigapascals, comparable to high-grade steel.
- Unlike steel, spider silk can stretch up to 27 percent of its length before breaking, giving it a toughness (energy absorbed before failure) roughly three times that of Kevlar.
- Researchers at Spiber Inc. in Tsuruoka, Japan, have engineered bacteria to produce recombinant spider silk proteins, which are then spun into fibers using an industrial wet-spinning process.
- The resulting synthetic fibers replicate about 80 percent of natural dragline silk''s mechanical properties.
- Current production costs for recombinant spider silk exceed 100 dollars per kilogram, compared to 2 dollars per kilogram for conventional nylon.
- Spiber projects that scaled fermentation will bring costs below 10 dollars per kilogram by 2029.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Spider dragline silk, with a tensile strength of approximately 1.1 gigapascals and the ability to stretch up to 27 percent of its length, is significantly tougher than Kevlar."},{"key":"B","text":"Although recombinant spider silk currently costs over 100 dollars per kilogram — far more than the 2-dollar-per-kilogram price of nylon — Spiber projects that scaled fermentation will reduce costs to below 10 dollars per kilogram by 2029."},{"key":"C","text":"Researchers at Spiber Inc. have engineered bacteria to produce recombinant spider silk proteins, which they spin into fibers using an industrial wet-spinning process that replicates about 80 percent of natural silk's mechanical properties."},{"key":"D","text":"Spiber Inc., based in Tsuruoka, Japan, is developing synthetic spider silk fibers that could eventually compete with nylon and Kevlar in commercial markets."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The dual goal requires acknowledging a limitation and conveying optimism. The sentence beginning "Although recombinant spider silk currently costs" accomplishes both by conceding the high current production cost relative to nylon (the limitation) and then citing Spiber''s specific projection of sub-10-dollar-per-kilogram costs by 2029 (the optimistic outlook). The sentence about tensile strength and stretch describes natural spider silk''s impressive mechanical properties but addresses neither a limitation of the synthetic version nor any commercial prospect. The sentence about Spiber engineering bacteria and replicating 80 percent of natural properties describes the production method and current performance level but does not frame the 20 percent gap as a limitation or express optimism about future improvement. The sentence about Spiber developing fibers that could compete with nylon and Kevlar gestures vaguely at a commercial future but does not specify any current limitation, making it one-sided rather than fulfilling the dual rhetorical goal.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 95, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


- Peregrine falcons were nearly extinct in the eastern United States by 1965 due to DDT-related eggshell thinning.
- After DDT was banned in 1972, wildlife agencies began releasing captive-bred falcons on tall buildings in major cities.
- Today over 70 nesting pairs occupy skyscrapers and bridges in New York, Philadelphia, and Baltimore.
- Urban peregrines hunt pigeons and starlings, which are abundant year-round in cities.
- The urban falcon population now exceeds pre-DDT rural population estimates for the same region.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"After DDT was banned in 1972, wildlife agencies released captive-bred peregrine falcons on tall buildings in major cities to help the species recover."},{"key":"B","text":"Peregrine falcons living in cities such as New York, Philadelphia, and Baltimore prey on pigeons and starlings, which are available throughout the year."},{"key":"C","text":"Once nearly extinct in the eastern United States, peregrine falcons have rebounded so successfully in cities that urban nesting pairs now outnumber the region's historical rural population."},{"key":"D","text":"Peregrine falcon populations declined sharply by 1965 because DDT caused their eggshells to thin, making successful reproduction nearly impossible."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The goal is to emphasize the surprising extent of the urban recovery. The sentence beginning "Once nearly extinct" accomplishes this by juxtaposing the falcons'' near-extinction with the fact that their urban population now exceeds pre-DDT rural numbers, directly highlighting the scale of the reversal. The sentence about wildlife agencies releasing captive-bred falcons describes a recovery method but does not convey the surprising scale of the outcome. The sentence about pigeons and starlings as prey describes a dietary detail rather than the recovery''s extent. The sentence about DDT-related decline addresses the cause of the original population crash without mentioning the recovery at all.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




Text 2: Geochemist Lena Haraldsen acknowledges that Siberian volcanism played a role in the end-Permian extinction but emphasizes that the eruptions alone cannot explain the event''s severity. Her isotopic analyses of marine sediments suggest that volcanic gases ignited vast underground coal deposits, multiplying carbon emissions far beyond what the magma itself produced. Without this secondary combustion, Haraldsen contends, the extinction would have been significantly less catastrophic.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing that volcanism was involved but arguing that an additional combustion process was necessary to produce the extinction's full severity."},{"key":"B","text":"By rejecting the volcanic hypothesis entirely and proposing an asteroid impact as the primary cause."},{"key":"C","text":"By questioning whether the Permian extinction actually occurred 252 million years ago."},{"key":"D","text":"By asserting that ocean acidification was irrelevant to the extinction."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 2''s author acknowledges that Siberian volcanism played a role but argues that the eruptions ignited underground coal deposits, multiplying carbon emissions and making the extinction far worse than volcanism alone could have caused. The option about agreeing with volcanism but adding a combustion process accurately describes this response. The option about rejecting the volcanic hypothesis and proposing an asteroid impact is wrong because Text 2 explicitly accepts volcanism as a contributing factor. The option about questioning the dating of the extinction is wrong because Text 2 does not dispute the timeline. The option about asserting ocean acidification was irrelevant is wrong because Text 2 does not address ocean acidification''s relevance one way or the other; its focus is on the amplifying role of coal combustion.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);







Paragraph 2: Yet recent studies by botanist Adriaan Patrut and colleagues have revealed that nine of the thirteen oldest known baobabs have partially or entirely died since 2005. While the researchers caution that no single cause has been conclusively identified, the pattern coincides with a period of unprecedented warming and altered rainfall cycles across southern Africa. The loss of trees estimated to be between 1,100 and 2,500 years old has prompted urgent calls for systematic monitoring of baobab populations continent-wide.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It qualifies the resilience described in the first paragraph by presenting evidence that even the hardiest baobabs may be vulnerable to recent environmental changes."},{"key":"B","text":"It refutes the first paragraph's characterization of the baobab as essential to local communities by showing the trees are dying."},{"key":"C","text":"It provides a biographical profile of a researcher whose work contradicts the claims made in the first paragraph."},{"key":"D","text":"It shifts from a scientific discussion to an emotional appeal for conservation funding."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The first paragraph establishes the baobab''s extraordinary drought resilience, and the second paragraph introduces "Yet" to pivot toward evidence that even these remarkably hardy trees are dying at an alarming rate, coinciding with unprecedented warming. The option about qualifying the resilience by presenting vulnerability evidence accurately captures this relationship: the second paragraph does not deny the baobab''s water-storage capacity but shows that even that capacity may be insufficient against current environmental pressures. The option about refuting the characterization as essential to communities is wrong because the deaths of baobabs would, if anything, confirm rather than refute their importance to communities. The option about providing a biographical profile misreads the paragraph: Patrut is mentioned as a researcher whose team produced findings, not as the subject of a biographical sketch. The option about shifting to an emotional appeal mischaracterizes the tone: the call for monitoring is presented as a scientific response, not an emotional plea for funding.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



